        <title>Claude Graft</title>
        <item>
            <title>1.1.0</title>
            <pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:14:36 +0530</pubDate>
            <link>https://github.com/aaditya-v-more/claude-graft</link>
            <sparkle:version>1.1.0</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.1.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumAutoupdateVersion>1.1.0</sparkle:minimumAutoupdateVersion>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>13.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Sharing a history now merges it</h2>

<p>Until now, pointing one profile at another's chats replaced its own. Your
conversations went into a hidden folder beside them and the other profile's
stood in their place, and a profile sharing chats this way could not archive,
rename or delete any of them — Claude will not write a record into a folder
that lives outside the profile.</p>

<p>From this version the folder is real and the two histories are merged, so
both sidebars hold both sets and a shared profile behaves like a profile
again.</p>

<h3>Worth reading before you install this one</h3>

<p><strong>Merging cannot be undone.</strong> The first launch after updating
copies each sharing profile's conversations into the profile it borrows from.
Sending it back to its own chats afterwards gives back everything it started
with, but the copies stay where they were merged.</p>

<p><strong>Take a copy first if that matters to you.</strong> Every profile
lives in <code>~/Library/Application Support</code>; copying the
<code>Claude</code> folders somewhere safe is the whole backup, and it takes a
minute.</p>

<p>Nothing is thrown away on the way. What each profile brought to the merge is
kept in a hidden folder beside its chats, which is what going back reads from.
And if a conversation does go missing, it is almost always still on disk: the
transcripts are in <code>~/.claude/projects</code>, and Graft files a record
back for any session that lost one.</p>

<p>This is the only update that will ask. Later ones install themselves
quietly, the way they always have.</p>
]]></description>
